.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt GROWROOT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm growroot
.Nd grow a root partition and its ext2 file system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar MBRID=ID,N
.Op Fl -measure
.Nm
.Ar GPTID=GUID,N
.Op Fl -measure
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
command grows a root partition and its ext2 file system into the free
space after it.
The operand identifies the disk and the partition as the kernel's
.Ql rach2.root=
operand does: an MBR disk by its hexadecimal signature
.Ar ID ,
or a disk with a GUID table by the table's disk GUID
.Ar GUID
in its 36-character text form, and the partition by entry number
.Ar N .
On an MBR disk the entry must be numbered 1 through 4, and its type must
be 0x83; on a GUID table it must be numbered 1 through 15, and its type
must be a Linux file system's, 0FC63DAF-8483-4772-8E79-3D69D8477DE4.
Exactly one disk must have the identity, which
.Xr diskctl 8
prints.
.Pp
On an MBR disk, growth is limited by the next partition's start, the end
of the disk, or the MBR limit of 2^32 sectors, whichever comes first.
A logical partition after the entry stops the growth, because its
records lie in the space the partition would occupy after growth.
On a GUID table, growth is limited by the next partition's start or the
sector before the backup table, placed at the disk's last 33 sectors, and
the backup is moved there in the same write if it is elsewhere, as
.Ql diskctl relocate
moves it.
Another used entry that starts inside the partition and ends past it lies
in the space the growth would take, and stops the growth: the kernel
publishes only the first of two overlapping entries in table order and
refuses the other, so nothing else would keep the file system from
growing over it.
An entry already at that size, on a table whose backup is already at the
disk's end, is left unchanged.
Nothing is moved and no other partition is changed.
.Pp
The steps are:
.Bl -enum
.It
Read the table again through the descriptor that writes it, and check
that the identity, the entry and the space it grows into are what was
decided.
Rewrite the partition entry with the new sector count.
On a GUID table, write the backup entry array and header at the disk's
end and then the primary array and header, each with its CRC32, so that
after any part of the writes one copy passes its checksums; when only the
backup passed, the primary is written first, for the same reason.
Writing a whole-disk node requires the recovery kernel.
.It
Ask the kernel to read the table again with
.Dv BLKRRPART .
The request is refused while the disk is in use.
Check that the table now reports the new size.
.It
Run
.Ql e2fsck -f -n
on the volume.
This check writes nothing.
If it returns a nonzero status,
.Nm
exits and reports that the volume must be repaired first.
.It
Run
.Ql e2fsck -f -p .
A status other than 0, 1 or 2 stops the run before the file system is
grown.
.It
Run
.Ql resize2fs
to grow the file system to the partition's size.
.El
.Pp
The table change is announced as
.Ql growroot: entry: ,
the checks as
.Ql growroot: check:
and the resize as
.Ql growroot: grow: .
A run stopped after changing the entry can be started again;
it finds the entry already at its new size and continues with the checks
and resize.
A damaged volume is not repaired by this tool: on a nonzero status from
the read-only check,
.Nm
exits and reports that the volume must be repaired first.
.Pp
Before reading the disks,
.Nm
exits if
.Ql MemTotal
is less than 7 MiB.
The file system checker and resizer keep the bitmaps in memory.
.Pp
The
.Fl -measure
option records monotonic microseconds and the command and flush counts
from
.Pa /proc/disk
around the table change and resize.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with status 1 if it rejects the operation or an operation fails.
For invalid syntax, it exits with status 2 and prints:
.Dl usage: growroot MBRID=ID,N | GPTID=GUID,N [--measure]
.Sh EXAMPLES
Grow primary entry 3 on the disk with MBR signature 1acef00d:
.Dl growroot MBRID=1acef00d,3
Grow entry 3 of the GUID table an installed disk has:
.Dl growroot GPTID=1acef00d-0f1e-4d2c-8b3a-c0ffee000001,3
The installer invokes this command when root growth is requested, with
the identity
.Ql diskctl id
prints.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Refusals are written to standard error as
.Ql growroot: WHAT: DETAIL .
Reported details include:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql the entry is not a primary one; only entries 1 to 4 grow
The entry number is outside the primary table of an MBR.
.It Ql the entry is past the fifteen partitions the kernel publishes
The entry number is past the partitions the kernel publishes.
.It Ql not found on any of N disks
No disk has the requested identity.
.It Ql ambiguous: more than one disk has that signature
The signature does not identify one disk; for a GUID it is
.Ql that GUID .
.It Ql not a Linux file system's entry
The GUID table's entry has another type.
.It Ql the table changed while this was deciding; nothing written
The table read through the writer's descriptor differs from the one the
decision was made on.
.It Ql entry N reaches into the space after it; nothing written
Another used entry of the GUID table starts inside the partition and ends
past it.
.It Ql a partition reaches past where the backup table would go
A GUID table's partition lies in the disk's last 33 sectors.
.It Ql the whole-disk node does not reach the disk's last sector
The node opened is shorter than the size
.Pa /proc/disk
gives the disk.
.It Ql N MiB of memory; growing a root needs 7 MiB
The machine has insufficient memory.
.It Ql the table write was refused; only the recovery kernel writes a whole disk
The kernel refused the table write.
.It Ql the table was written but not read again: the disk is in use
The kernel refused to reread the table.
.It Ql the table read again does not show the new size
The reread table does not report the new sector count.
.It Ql e2fsck -fn status N; repair it first, since a repair writes outside the saved set
The read-only check failed.
.It Ql e2fsck status N; the volume is not grown
The subsequent check failed.
.It Ql resize2fs status N
The resize failed.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr disk 7 ,
.Xr diskctl 8 ,
.Xr e2fsck 8 ,
.Xr rach2-install 8 ,
.Xr resize2fs 8
